WEBVTT

afferent-light
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.600
Light entering the eye starts a signal in the afferent, or sensory pathway.

afferent-brain
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:24.233
Follow the coloured pathways (retina, optic nerve, chiasm, tract and nuclei).

efferent-definition
00:00:24.233 --> 00:00:40.233
Efferent signals go from the brain to the eye. There are two pathways, parasympathetic and sympathetic. Parasympathetic signals activate the constrictor muscle and sympathetic signals activate the dilator muscle.

parasympathetic
00:00:40.233 --> 00:00:50.233
The third cranial nerve also carries the efferent signals for pupil constriction.

constrictor
00:00:50.233 --> 00:00:57.233
It supplies the iris sphincter to constrict the pupil and the ciliary muscle for focusing.

third-nerve
00:00:57.233 --> 00:01:04.233
The third nerve controls most eye movements and lifts the lid. The facial nerve controls blinking.

sympathetic
00:01:04.233 --> 00:01:14.233
The sympathetic pathway passes through the sympathetic chain and the superior cervical ganglion.

dilator
00:01:14.233 --> 00:01:24.233
It supplies two muscles. First, the iris dilator muscle. Second, Müller's muscle, a minor elevator of the upper eyelid.

reflex-input
00:01:27.233 --> 00:01:35.233
Light in one eye sends an afferent signal towards the brain.

reflex-output
00:01:35.233 --> 00:01:43.233
Signals from the midbrain reach both eyes, so both pupils constrict in response to light.

reflex-direct
00:01:43.233 --> 00:01:46.733
The illuminated pupil has a direct response.

reflex-consensual
00:01:46.733 --> 00:01:51.233
The other pupil has a consensual response.

observe
00:01:54.233 --> 00:02:00.233
Observe the pupils. They should be black, round and equal in size.

compare-observation
00:02:00.233 --> 00:02:12.233
Check for changes in pupil colour or shape, unequal pupil size and drooping eyelids.

light
00:02:15.233 --> 00:02:24.233
Switch on the Arclight and select the brightest light setting. Use a quiet, dim room.

distance
00:02:24.233 --> 00:02:30.233
Ask the patient to look at a distant target.

clear-view
00:02:30.233 --> 00:02:42.233
Make sure they do not look at the examiner or the light itself.

move-light
00:02:42.233 --> 00:02:48.233
Move the light from the side towards the pupil.

watch
00:02:48.233 --> 00:02:54.233
Watch the illuminated pupil for the direct light response.

shine-constrict
00:02:54.233 --> 00:03:00.233
Shine the light into the eye and watch the pupil constrict.

compare-responses
00:03:00.233 --> 00:03:06.233
In a healthy eye, the pupil constricts normally.

corresponding-examples
00:03:06.233 --> 00:03:14.233
These are possible associations. The pupil response alone does not identify the underlying eye condition.

pupil-colour
00:03:14.233 --> 00:03:22.233
A grey or white appearance does not by itself mean the pupil cannot constrict.

cataract-constriction
00:03:22.233 --> 00:03:38.233
If the response is reduced in an eye with a cataract, consider coexisting disease of the retina, the optic nerve or both.

consensual-first
00:03:41.233 --> 00:03:49.233
Shine light into one eye and watch the other pupil constrict. This is the consensual response.

consensual-other
00:03:49.233 --> 00:04:03.233
Repeat on the other side and watch the pupil in the eye without the light.

swing-method
00:04:06.233 --> 00:04:16.233
Move the light between the eyes, pausing for two to three seconds on each side. Compare both pupils.

swing-return
00:04:16.233 --> 00:04:26.233
Swing the light back and watch both pupils as you change sides.

swing-normal
00:04:26.233 --> 00:04:36.233
Normally, both pupils constrict equally when either eye is illuminated.

swing-rapd
00:04:36.233 --> 00:04:50.233
A relative afferent pupillary defect, or RAPD, means one eye sends a weaker light signal than the other.
Here, both pupils dilate when the light moves to the affected right eye.

near-distance
00:04:53.233 --> 00:04:58.608
First ask the patient to look at a distant target.

near-target
00:04:58.608 --> 00:05:06.100
Then ask them to look at a near target on the Arclight.

near-normal
00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:16.100
The eyes turn inwards to fix on the near target. This convergence accompanies pupil constriction and lens accommodation.

near-abnormal
00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:26.100
If the eyes do not turn inwards together for a near target, further examination is needed.
